From: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
Date: Sun, 21 Sep 2026 22:40:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: honour the hardware RX checksum status

airoha_qdma_build_rx_skb() stamps every non-LRO frame with
CHECKSUM_UNNECESSARY without ever looking at the per-packet checksum
status the frame engine already reports. RX MSG1 (RXD4) carries
QDMA_ETH_RXMSG_L4_VALID_MASK, QDMA_ETH_RXMSG_IP4F_MASK and
QDMA_ETH_RXMSG_L4F_MASK right next to the IP4/IP6 type bits the LRO path
already trusts, but all three defines are dead code: nothing in the
driver reads them. They have been unused since the driver was merged in
23020f049327 ("net: airoha: Introduce ethernet support for EN7581 SoC").

The Airoha frame engine keeps the MediaTek field names and order, and
MediaTek documents them. MT7620 Programming Guide v1.3, section 2.18.7.1
"PDMA Rx Field Descriptions", DWORD3 PKT_INFO, p.206:

  Bit[4]: IPv4 packet
  Bit[3]: IPV4 checksum error
  Bit[1]: L4 TCP or UDP packet without fragmented flag
  Bit[0]: L4 checksum error

So L4_VALID reports only that an L4 checksum was evaluated at all - the
frame is non-fragmented TCP or UDP - and IP4F/L4F are the failure flags.
The correct predicate for CHECKSUM_UNNECESSARY is therefore
L4_VALID && !(IP4F | L4F), which is what both the vendor raeth driver
and mtk_eth_soc (rx.dma_l4_valid) use.

Today the unconditional claim mostly holds by accident of a register
written once at init: FWD_CFG's IP4_CKSUM/TCP_CKSUM/UDP_CKSUM bits make
the GDM parser drop failing frames before they are DMAed, so a frame that
reaches this function has generally been verified. That leaves two holes.
Anything for which the engine evaluates no L4 checksum - IPv4/IPv6
fragments, ICMP, SCTP, GRE/ESP - is still handed to the stack as
verified, and for SCTP the kernel then skips CRC32c entirely. And the
accident stops holding the moment those three bits are cleared, which is
what an ethtool -K <dev> rx off is supposed to do and what is needed to
inspect the frames the parser rejects: frames with a bad L4 checksum are
then accepted without any verification.

Read the status bits, and fall back to CHECKSUM_NONE unless the engine
says it computed an L4 checksum and that checksum passed, or when RX
checksum offload is off for the netdev. Normal TCP and UDP traffic keeps
CHECKSUM_UNNECESSARY, so the fast path is unchanged; it costs one extra
descriptor field read.

Signed-off-by: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
@@ -884,6 +884,7 @@
 	} else {
 		u32 desc_ctrl = le32_to_cpu(READ_ONCE(desc->ctrl));
 		u32 len = FIELD_GET(QDMA_DESC_LEN_MASK, desc_ctrl);
+		u32 msg1 = le32_to_cpu(READ_ONCE(desc->msg1));
 
 		skb = napi_build_skb(e->buf - AIROHA_RX_HEADROOM, q->buf_size);
 		if (!skb)
@@ -891,7 +892,12 @@
 
 		skb_reserve(skb, AIROHA_RX_HEADROOM);
 		__skb_put(skb, len);
-		skb->ip_summed = CHECKSUM_UNNECESSARY;
+		if ((netdev->features & NETIF_F_RXCSUM) &&
+		    (msg1 & QDMA_ETH_RXMSG_L4_VALID_MASK) &&
+		    !(msg1 & QDMA_ETH_RXMSG_CKSUM_FAIL_MASK))
+			skb->ip_summed = CHECKSUM_UNNECESSARY;
+		else
+			skb_checksum_none_assert(skb);
 	}
 
 	skb_mark_for_recycle(skb);
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_regs.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_regs.h
@@ -910,6 +910,8 @@
 #define QDMA_ETH_RXMSG_IP4F_MASK	BIT(28)
 #define QDMA_ETH_RXMSG_L4_VALID_MASK	BIT(27)
 #define QDMA_ETH_RXMSG_L4F_MASK		BIT(26)
+#define QDMA_ETH_RXMSG_CKSUM_FAIL_MASK	(QDMA_ETH_RXMSG_IP4F_MASK | \
+					 QDMA_ETH_RXMSG_L4F_MASK)
 #define QDMA_ETH_RXMSG_SPORT_MASK	GENMASK(25, 21)
 #define QDMA_ETH_RXMSG_CRSN_MASK	GENMASK(20, 16)
 #define QDMA_ETH_RXMSG_PPE_ENTRY_MASK	GENMASK(15, 0)
